{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791315426,"nonce":"790bIqZXlloKnmBPjEVjojWpV3k=","message":"Compared Zyntax 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b with sibling Ash e217f5d (local checkout).\n\nNormal Zyntax single-function MCJIT promotion already creates a fresh LLVMBackend/module. compile_function includes one root, its referenced extern declarations, requested OSR helpers, and only referenced globals. Other JIT functions use stable cross-tier call cells. The HIR module is Arc-shared; the mutable LLVM module is not. ZYNTAX_LLVM_CLOSURE_PROMOTION or the non-MCJIT fallback instead includes the callee closure. ZyntaxLlvmBackend holds one mutex across the whole compile, so additional Beadie workers alone cannot parallelize this context.\n\nAsh's promote_uses_own_module chooses private modules for lazy compilation or when AIR sees no useful remaining callees, and caps the shared module at 256 bodies. The shared path budgets callee lowering (up to 64), parks previously optimized functions, and refuses unsuitable promotions. Private promotions seed only their own constants, materializing further referenced constants on demand. Zyntax already avoids the growing shared-module reoptimization and whole-global-pool costs in its normal path.\n\nThe concrete remaining retention difference: Ash promotes private modules through a persistent execution_engine, uniquely renames each entry, resolves declarations by function identity, then calls remove_module after obtaining all code addresses. This lets the IR drop while the engine keeps emitted code. Zyntax creates a new engine per install, keeps it in engines, and never removes its module IR. Reusing one engine requires unique install symbols for repeated entries and OSR helpers, tested old-pointer lifetime and globals, and preserving cross-tier ABI/call-cell behavior. Merely sharing a mutable module would reintroduce the cost Ash is avoiding.\n\nFiles: Zyntax llvm_jit_backend.rs compile_function / compile_module_to_ir / install_via_mcjit; beadie_adapter.rs ZyntaxLlvmBackend. Ash crates/ash/src/llvm/function/mod.rs promote_uses_own_module / promote_in_own_module / release_module_ir; crates/ash/src/llvm/air.rs promotion_wants_full_module. The Ash llvm_worker_pool CLI test exercises Haxe worker execution and stack availability, not parallel LLVM contexts; its tiering state still has a global LLVM mutex.","files":null}]}